+### PS Vita
+
+`THPS_ARCH=vita` cross-compiles with [VitaSDK](https://vitasdk.org) (`VITASDK`, default
+`/usr/local/vitasdk`; its SDL2 and vitaGL) and packages a VPK:
+
+```
+export VITASDK=/usr/local/vitasdk
+THPS_ARCH=vita python3 platform/pc/tools/build.py   # -> build/vita/thps.vpk (title ID THPS00001)
+```
+
+The build needs the same inputs as the PC ones (`extern/thps1_retail/SLUS_008.60`, the GNU v2
+demangler, `build/psyq_names.tsv`). To run it on the console:
+
+- Install `thps.vpk` with VitaShell.
+- Put the disc image in `ux0:data/THPS/` as `thps1.bin` (or `Tony Hawk's Pro Skater (USA).bin`).
+  The first start extracts `Data/` next to it (about 630 MB; the image can be deleted afterwards).
+- vitaGL compiles the renderer's shaders at runtime: `ur0:data/libshacccg.suprx` must be present
+  (extracted from the firmware with ShaRKBR33D or the like).
+- `ux0:data/THPS/thps.log` is the log (written straight through, so it survives a crash);
+  `ux0:data/THPS/env.txt` holds `NAME=value` lines that stand in for environment variables
+  (`THPS_TRACE=1` ...). Saves go in `ux0:data/THPS` too.
+
+How the Vita build differs:
+
+- ARM EABI: game code and data are compiled with `-fno-short-enums` (the game's structs have
+  4-byte enums) and `-fpcc-struct-return` (aggregates returned through memory, as egcs on MIPS
+  does). The Cortex-A9 has no divide instruction; the EABI division helpers are wrapped
+  (`src/pc_vita.cpp`) to give the R3000's division-by-zero results.
+- There are no fault handlers: null and near-null reads the x86 builds redirect to the low page
+  are routed there by `PC_LOW()` (`include/pc_game.h`) at the sites they were found at. A new
+  one crashes the Vita build; on Linux it shows in the log as a "low-memory access" line.
+- The renderer is the OpenGL ES 2 one (`include/pc/gl_shaders_gles.h`, `PC_GLES`): the same
+  pipeline as the GL 3.3 one, with float palette lookups from a luminance-alpha texture store,
+  depth from clip space instead of `gl_FragDepth`, and blending without dual-source outputs. It
+  draws at 1x (320x240) and scales to the 960x544 screen. No shadows or MSAA.
+  `THPS_GLES=1` builds it for Linux (with its own `THPS_BUILD_DIR`) to test it on a desktop.
+
